--TEST--
`$uri->compareTo` is bounded to exactly `-1`, `0` and `1` over every ordered pair of a corpus, and it is the only ordering two `Uri`s have: `Core\Arr`'s natural order refuses one at `min`, `max` and `sort` alike, and the way through is `{comparator: ...}` calling this member — spec § 12, ADR 0013, ADR 0090 § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One neighbouring case shows this member is a total order, another that it
// decides on the normal forms. Neither asks what the answer may *be*: an order
// that is antisymmetric, reflexive and transitive is still all three when it
// answers a byte difference, which is what PHP's `strcasecmp` does to this day
// and what `Core\Str::compare` had to be normalized away from. So the bound is
// counted here, over every ordered pair of a corpus rather than over a chosen
// row, and all three values are required to occur — a member answering `0`
// everywhere satisfies the bound and nothing else.
array<string> $corpus = [
    "http://a/",
    "http://b/",
    "HTTP://A/",
    "https://a/",
    "http://a/z",
    "http://a/#f",
];
int $pairs = 0;
int $bounded = 0;
int $below = 0;
int $same = 0;
int $above = 0;
foreach ($corpus as string $left) {
    foreach ($corpus as string $right) {
        $pairs = $pairs + 1;
        int $answer = Core\Uri::parse($left)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($right));
        if ($answer == -1 || $answer == 0 || $answer == 1) {
            $bounded = $bounded + 1;
        }
        if ($answer == -1) { $below = $below + 1; }
        if ($answer == 0) { $same = $same + 1; }
        if ($answer == 1) { $above = $above + 1; }
    }
}
echo "pairs ", $pairs, " bounded ", $bounded, "\n";
echo "below ", $below, " same ", $same, " above ", $above, "\n";

// `same` is above the six the reflexive rows account for, because the corpus
// holds one pair that is two spellings of one reference: `HTTP://A/` and
// `http://a/` normalize together under RFC 3986 § 6.2.2, and this member is
// the only place that is observable — ADR 0090 § 4 keeps `==` on two objects
// meaning the same object, so the two spellings are never `==`.
//
// § 6.2.2 is the *syntax-based* normalization and stops there, which is why
// the corpus does not write `http://a:80/` as a third spelling of the same
// reference: dropping a scheme's default port is § 6.2.3, this member does
// not do it, and an absent port sorts before a written one exactly as the
// neighbouring total-order case has an absent component sort before an empty
// one.
var $written = Core\Uri::parse("HTTP://A/");
var $plain = Core\Uri::parse("http://a/");
echo $written->compareTo($plain), " ", $written->toString(), "\n";

// And it is the *only* order they have. `Core\Arr`'s natural order is the
// scalar one — two numbers, two strings, two bools or two nulls — so an
// object in the subject is refused rather than dispatched to `Comparable`,
// at each of the three members that reach for it. The throw is catchable,
// which is what lets the three refusals render inline here.
array<mixed> $subject = [$plain, $written];
try { echo Core\Arr::min($subject), "\n"; } catch (Throwable $atMin) { echo "min refused\n"; }
try { echo Core\Arr::max($subject), "\n"; } catch (Throwable $atMax) { echo "max refused\n"; }
try { var $s = Core\Arr::sort($subject); echo "sorted\n"; } catch (Throwable $atSort) { echo "sort refused\n"; }

// The way through is the option that member's own message names, and what a
// comparator writes is this member: three values are all an order needs, so
// the bound counted above is not a limitation to work around. Sorting the
// texts rather than the parsed references keeps the answer printable, and it
// is the order the counts above already imply — the two spellings of one
// reference land adjacent, in the order they were given.
var $ordered = Core\Arr::sort($corpus, {comparator: fn (string $x, string $y): int => Core\Uri::parse($x)->compareTo(Core\Uri::parse($y))});
echo Core\Json::encode($ordered), "\n";
--EXPECT--
pairs 36 bounded 36
below 14 same 8 above 14
0 HTTP://A/
min refused
max refused
sort refused
["http://a/","HTTP://A/","http://a/#f","http://a/z","http://b/","https://a/"]
